00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:12.500
When the United States gets hungry, and, we
do that a lot, we like to hop in our car,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.160
preferably not get out of our car, and pay
an absurdly small amount of money.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:23.869
Fast food, or Quick Service restaurants, are
a $256 billion a year business made one or

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:26.489
two or five dollars at a time.

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:35.649
Globally, there are 20,000 KFCs, 30,000 Starbucks,
37,000 McDonalds, and, more than any other,

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:37.070
43,000 Subways.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:43.379
There’s one in every U.S. state, every U.S.
territory except American Samoa, schools,

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:44.859
churches, and casinos.

00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:49.980
One, even, was hydraulically-lifted above
New York’s Freedom Tower during its construction

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.829
to accommodate workers who didn’t have enough
time during their lunch break to go down to

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:54.989
ground level.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:01:00.460
Subway, like many companies, got so big by
outsourcing its growth - allowing just about

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.379
anyone, just about anywhere to open their
own location.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:11.940
It’s the poster child for franchising - growing
22% during the worst recession in 75 years.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.990
But it’s also a perfect example of what
can go terribly wrong.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:24.119
In 2017, Subway lost over 900 U.S. stores,
and the $5 footlong, the catalyst behind the

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:27.310
company’s insane growth, became the source
of its downfall.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.829
So, why do some companies choose to share
their business model when they could just

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:36.470
keep all the profit themselves?

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:41.299
If you a) like the idea of being your own
boss and b) know how to cook, you might be

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:43.329
tempted to start a restaurant.

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:48.570
Something like 1,000 new independent restaurants
open each year in the U.S. and it’s easy

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:49.930
to see why.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:54.050
The average American spends 13% of their income
on food.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.289
Nearly half of which is eating out.

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:01.400
Gyms and travel agencies are fickle, but almost
everyone needs to eat.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.659
And starting a restaurant can cost as little
as $100,000, which, while not exactly cheap,

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.750
is relatively accessible for many people,
with loans.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.280
But, of course, it’s much easier said than
done.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.980
The idea of not having a boss sounds great
until you realize that means working long

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:23.610
hours, getting paid last, and being responsible
for every little thing that goes wrong.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.240
People often choose to open a Mexican restaurant
because that’s what they like or a gym because

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.360
they like working out, but soon find that
managing a business and eating tacos are two,

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.590
very distinct skills.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:39.960
Restaurants also have extremely high turnover,
and often employ family members, which makes

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.920
firing them especially difficult.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.100
It’s a myth that 90% of restaurants fail
in the first year, but the truth is still

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.070
not very promising - about a third in the
first year and two thirds after three.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.790
Although, this does include change of ownership,
and sometimes owners are just ready to move

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.790
on.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.670
Regardless, it’s not an easy feat.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:04.350
So, if you do succeed, you tend to get a little…
confident.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.960
If you can turn a hundred thousand dollar
loan into a hundred thousand profit in a few

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.580
years, why stop there?

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.740
A second restaurant could double your income!

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.540
At least, because now you have experience!

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.790
The truth is, no matter how successful you
are, you haven’t yet proven the business

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:21.790
model.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:27.340
You’ve proven the business model at this
location, with these employees, this year.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.030
Breaking into a new market isn’t easy, even
for the biggest companies on earth.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:38.210
Amazon failed in China, Uber in Korea, and,
for a long time, Dunkin’ in California.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:44.400
A smart business owner knows each and every
new location is a huge opportunity and a huge

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.400
risk.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.790
Enter: the franchise!

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:52.070
It’s easy to see a company like Subway as
an unstoppable giant, but one day it was just

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:56.450
a 17-year-old kid in Connecticut trying to
pay his way through medical school and opening

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.600
a second location meant gambling everything.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.510
The beauty of franchising for the Franchisor,
that is, the larger company, is that it shifts

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:09.570
some of that risk to the Franchisee, the person
who pays to open a new location, and usually

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:13.130
buys or leases the land, building, and equipment.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:16.769
Every franchise is different, but here’s
how it generally works:

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:21.280
First, anyone who meets the minimum requirements
can apply to open a new location.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.610
The potential franchisee and an agent representing
the franchisor will then interview each other,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.620
deciding whether it’s a good fit.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:33.110
This culminates in what’s called a Discovery
Day - where the candidate flies to the company’s

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:37.770
headquarters, meets its executives, and see
its operations firsthand before making a final

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:38.770
decision.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.669
If both parties are satisfied, they sign a
Franchise Agreement - which has three main

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:44.689
components.

00:04:44.689 --> 00:04:46.840
First - it sets the length of the contract.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:52.550
A franchise is really a lease, and when it
expires, usually in 5-10 years, both parties

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.389
have to agree to renew it.

00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:57.979
Otherwise, the franchisee is simply out of
luck.

00:04:57.979 --> 00:04:59.469
Second - it outlines the costs.

00:04:59.469 --> 00:05:06.189
There’s an upfront, one-time Franchise Fee,
think $10-50,000, and an ongoing royalty,

00:05:06.189 --> 00:05:08.870
say 4-15% of all revenue.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.600
Finally, the Franchise Agreement lays out,
in great detail, exact requirements for doing

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.509
business, from how everything is made, what’s
on the menu, and often, what hours it must

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:21.150
be open, sometimes on Christmas.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:26.449
The goal is to maintain consistency - every
McDonalds and Subway and Dunkin’ should

00:05:26.449 --> 00:05:28.610
look, smell, and feel the same.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.639
Without these strict standards, individual
stores could coast on the success of the brand

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:35.710
as a whole, while cutting corners and saving
money.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:40.229
For the franchisee, this model is attractive
because it’s relatively safe.

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:44.710
If people like McDonald’s fries in D.C.,
so you can pretty safely bet they’ll also

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.941
like them in Portland.

00:05:46.941 --> 00:05:51.240
And, when the time comes, it’s much easier
to sell a Pizza Hut than a generic Bob’s

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.259
Local Pizza.

00:05:52.259 --> 00:05:56.310
You probably won’t be as rich as, say, a
successful tech startup, but you’ll also

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.129
sleep much better at night knowing that while
your income ceiling is much lower, the floor

00:06:01.129 --> 00:06:02.129
is much higher.

00:06:02.129 --> 00:06:07.949
The secret to Subway’s success is that stability
combined with low barriers to entry.

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:13.940
Its franchise fee is just $15,000, in total,
about a tenth of the cost to open a McDonalds.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:19.710
They’re also quite small - just 1,200 square
feet on average, compared to McDonald’s

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:20.710
4,500.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:27.210
In 2013, Subway opened 50 new locations a
week - that’s seven every single day, on

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.210
average.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:32.250
But don’t confuse Subway’s success with
its Franchisees’.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:36.090
Companies like to portray franchising as a
sort-of economic marriage - both wish the

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:39.919
other success, while the corporation provides
support and guidance.

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:42.599
The reality, though, is often much different.

00:06:42.599 --> 00:06:46.830
The local owner of a Subway, for example,
might argue that while he or she is in the

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:51.389
business of selling sandwiches, Subway, the
corporation, is really in the business of

00:06:51.389 --> 00:06:52.969
selling its logo.

00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:56.529
Because it doesn’t own any of its stores,
but makes a minimum of fifteen grand no matter

00:06:56.529 --> 00:07:01.259
how successful they are, it’s incentivized
to open lots of locations, regardless of how

00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:03.159
qualified the applicants.

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:08.360
Unless they have exclusive territory agreements,
longtime franchisees may find themselves competing

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.249
with another Subway or six within a mile radius.

00:07:12.249 --> 00:07:15.349
And they often find franchise agreements overly
restrictive.

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:19.500
Sure, you don’t have to come up with new
recipes or find the right equipment, just

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.080
follow the instructions, but you also don’t
get to if you have a better idea.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.120
This tension became an all-out war with the
$5 footlong.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.389
It started as a brilliant idea.

00:07:30.389 --> 00:07:35.830
Something about that clean, even $5 price
attracted customers like nothing else in company’s

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:36.830
history.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:43.289
Now, one would assume a $6 footlong would
sell about 20% worse, because, ya know, math!

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:47.779
But one would also assume no-one would eat
disgusting cashews and yet you goons gobble

00:07:47.779 --> 00:07:49.240
them up like crazy!

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:54.059
The point is people are weird, and in the
15 years since the $5 footlong was introduced,

00:07:54.059 --> 00:07:58.650
haven’t gotten any less weird, in fact,
weirder probably, but while we still like

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:04.180
nice, round numbers, because of inflation,
5 2004 dollars is now equivalent to about

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.389
$6.78.

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:11.779
Not to mention the rising cost of labor, land,
and health insurance, which also vary considerably

00:08:11.779 --> 00:08:12.779
by region.

00:08:12.779 --> 00:08:16.809
In other words, Subway is making a lot less
money for the same price.

00:08:16.809 --> 00:08:22.080
Well, Subway Franchisees, that is, because,
remember, Subway Incorporated takes a cut

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.159
of revenue, not profit.

00:08:24.159 --> 00:08:28.389
The promotion was great for the corporation,
it even threatened to sue other chains who

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:31.729
used the term “footlong” in advertising
the length of their sandwiches.

00:08:31.729 --> 00:08:36.370
And when an Australian teenager complained
that his sandwich was only 11 inches long,

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.410
Subway argued it’s “Footlong”, not foot-long.

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:43.020
Clearly it’s a descriptive name for the
product, not intended to be a measurement

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:44.020
of length.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:45.020
Duh!

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:50.390
In 2014, Subway switched to a $6 combo with
a drink and cookie or chips, but it wasn’t

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.520
nearly as successful.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:57.130
And, after much fighting, the company finally
made the $5 promotion optional for franchisees

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:58.130
in 2018.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:01.410
The damage, it seems, was already done.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:07.230
Subway fell into the franchise trap - focusing
only growth, to the detriment of its existing

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:08.230
locations.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.410
Meanwhile, people’s preferences simply moved
on.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:16.900
At their best, franchises represent the American
dream - anyone with an entrepreneurial appetite

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:21.130
and even the most modest savings can go from
working in a convenience store to owning one

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.520
in a matter of years.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:28.320
But, like anywhere money is made, there tend
to be a few big winners - the owners of 5,

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:34.300
10, 50 locations who’ve optimized for everything,
and many others barely scraping by, despite,

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:39.600
not uncommonly, making a million dollars in
revenue, per store, each year.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.270
Franchising can be a great opportunity, but
it’s important to remember that each new

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:49.350
location is a new risk - not just that it
will fail, but everything you own will.

